                        'biography' => "Anas ibn Malik, the personal attendant of the Prophet ﷺ, was among the most prolific narrators of his hadith. Born in Medina into Banu an-Najjar of the Khazraj ten years before the Hijrah, he was brought by his mother Umm Sulaym when the Prophet arrived: 'O Messenger of Allah, this is Anas, a boy to serve you' — and he served him for ten full years until the Prophet's death. Of those years he said: 'By Allah, he never once said to me uff! He never asked of anything I did, why did you do it? Nor of anything I left, why did you leave it?' The Prophet prayed for him: 'O Allah, increase his wealth and his children, and bless him in what You give him' — and he became among the wealthiest of the Ansar in property and offspring, burying a hundred and twenty of his own descendants and harvesting his orchard twice a year. A sharp and retentive boy, he served at Badr as a youth and witnessed the later campaigns after coming of age. He preserved immense knowledge — 2,286 hadiths, including the narration of the Night Journey and intimate details of the Prophet's character known only to one who lived in his house. After the conquests he settled in Basra as teacher and mufti, becoming the last companion to die there. He passed away in 93 AH, having exceeded a hundred years; with his death the age of companionship ended in Basra, and Muwarriq al-Ijli said: 'Today half of all knowledge has departed.'",
