                        'biography' => "Muadh ibn Jabal will be raised on the Day of Judgement at the forefront of the scholars, for the Prophet ﷺ testified: 'The most knowledgeable of my nation in the lawful and the forbidden is Muadh ibn Jabal.' Born in Medina into Banu Salimah of the Khazraj, he embraced Islam at eighteen and was among the seventy Ansar at the second Pledge of Aqabah. One of the most handsome and generous-natured young men of the Ansar, he compiled the Quran in the Prophet's lifetime and was one of the four of whom the Prophet said: 'Take the Quran from four.' He fought at Badr, Uhud, the Trench and every campaign. The Prophet loved him openly, telling him: 'O Muadh, by Allah I love you — so never leave saying after every prayer: O Allah, help me to remember You, thank You and worship You well.' Near the end of his life the Prophet sent him to Yemen as judge, teacher and caller, asking him: 'By what will you judge?' He answered: by the Book of Allah, then the Sunnah of His Messenger, then my own reasoned judgement — and the Prophet praised Allah for guiding 'the messenger of the Messenger of Allah'. Walking beside his mount in farewell, the Prophet said: 'O Muadh, perhaps you will not meet me after this year of mine.' He later went to Syria as fighter and teacher; when Abu Ubaidah died of the plague he succeeded him in command, then was himself taken by the plague of Amwas, dying in the Jordan Valley in 18 AH at about thirty-eight.",
