                        'biography' => "Abdullah ibn Umar, son of the second caliph, was the imam of scrupulousness and the companion most devoted to following the Prophet's every footstep. Born in Mecca about ten years before the Hijrah, he embraced Islam as a boy with his father and migrated with him to Medina. Presented before the Prophet at Badr and at Uhud, he was turned away as too young; his first battle was the Trench at fifteen, followed by the Pledge of Ridwan and the later campaigns. A dream he saw in his youth was interpreted by the Prophet: 'What an excellent man Abdullah is — if only he prayed at night,' and he never abandoned the night prayer thereafter. His exactness in emulation was unmatched: he prayed where the Prophet prayed, halted where he halted, and retraced his very routes — Aisha said no one followed the Prophet's footsteps in his stopping-places as Ibn Umar did. He narrated 2,630 hadiths, second only to Abu Hurairah, never issuing rulings beyond what he knew. He withdrew from every civil war between Muslims, saying: 'Whoever calls \"come to prayer\" I answer; whoever calls \"come to kill your Muslim brother\" — no.' Offered the caliphate more than once, he refused it, fleeing from bloodshed. He gave fatwas to the people for sixty years and freed a thousand slaves. He died in Mecca in 73 AH at eighty-four, the last companion to die there.",
