                        'biography' => "Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, the sixth person to embrace Islam, became the nation's imam in Quranic recitation and jurisprudence. A slight young shepherd tending the flocks of Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt near Mecca, he was asked for milk by the passing Prophet ﷺ and Abu Bakr; he refused — 'I am entrusted' — and his honesty delighted them. The Prophet stroked a barren ewe and it flowed with milk; the boy begged, 'Teach me of this speech,' and the Prophet said: 'You are a taught lad.' He embraced Islam and took seventy surahs directly from the Prophet's mouth. He was the first after the Prophet to recite the Quran aloud in Mecca, reading Surah ar-Rahman at the Kaaba while the idolaters beat his face bloody as he read on. He made both migrations and fought at Badr and every campaign. He was keeper of the Prophet's siwak, sandals, washing-water and screen — entering upon him so freely that strangers thought him one of the household. The Prophet said: 'Whoever wishes to recite the Quran fresh as it was revealed, let him recite it as Ibn Umm Abd recites'; and when companions laughed at his thin shins, he declared: 'By Him in Whose hand is my soul, they are heavier in the Scale than Mount Uhud.' Umar sent him to Kufa as teacher and minister — 'I have preferred you with him over myself' — where he founded the Kufan school of law whose line runs through Alqamah and al-Aswad to Ibrahim an-Nakha'i, Hammad and Abu Hanifah. He died in Medina in 32 AH and was buried in al-Baqi.",
                        'virtues' => "Sixth person to embrace Islam and first to recite the Quran publicly in Mecca after the Prophet. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Whoever wishes to recite the Quran fresh as it was revealed, let him recite as Ibn Umm Abd' — and that his thin shins outweigh Mount Uhud in the Scale. Founder of the Kufan school of fiqh.",
                        'famous_narrations' => "He narrated 848 hadiths, including: 'There is no envy except in two,' and 'Truthfulness leads to righteousness, and righteousness leads to Paradise.'",
