                        'biography' => "Abu Dharr Jundub ibn Junadah al-Ghifari, the fourth or fifth person to embrace Islam, is the imam of asceticism and of speaking truth aloud. A monotheist even in the pre-Islamic era who never worshipped idols, he sent his brother to investigate news of the Prophet in Mecca, then traveled himself, discreetly reaching the Prophet, hearing him, and believing on the spot. Told to conceal his Islam and return to his people, his outspoken nature refused: he stood in the sacred mosque and cried at the top of his voice, 'I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah' — and the idolaters beat him to the ground until al-Abbas rescued him; the next day he did the same again. Returning to Ghifar, he called them to Islam and half the tribe believed; he rejoined the Prophet after the Trench and never left him. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'The sky has not shaded nor the earth carried anyone more truthful of tongue than Abu Dharr.' Severely ascetic, he held that no Muslim should keep a dinar or dirham beyond his need, and when this view set him at odds with others in Syria during Uthman's caliphate, he sought leave and withdrew to ar-Rabadhah, worshipping there until death came in 32 AH — alone, as the Prophet had foretold: 'May Allah have mercy on Abu Dharr: he walks alone, dies alone, and will be resurrected alone.' A passing caravan that included Abdullah ibn Mas'ud prayed over him.",
