                        'laqab' => 'Mother of the Believers, the Wise',
                        'biography' => "Umm Salamah, Hind bint Abi Umayyah, Mother of the Believers, was among the most excellent women of the worlds in intellect, religion and beauty. Born in Mecca into Banu Makhzum, daughter of the famously generous Abu Umayyah 'Provision of the Caravan', she and her husband Abu Salamah were among the earliest Muslims, making both migrations to Abyssinia. She was then among the first women to migrate to Medina — a migration of great trial, separated from husband and child for nearly a year, weeping each morning in the valley until her family was reunited. When Abu Salamah died of his Uhud wound, she spoke the words the Prophet had taught: 'O Allah, reward me in my calamity and replace it for me with something better' — wondering to herself, who could be better than Abu Salamah? Allah replaced him with His own Messenger ﷺ, who married her in 4 AH. Her judgement was among the soundest in Islam's history: at Hudaybiyyah, when the Muslims, grieved by the treaty, hesitated to end their pilgrim state, she counseled the Prophet to go out, sacrifice his animal and shave his head without a word — he did, and the people rushed to follow. She narrated 378 hadiths and was a reference for companions and successors in fiqh, especially the rulings of women. Living long after the Prophet through the eras of the caliphs, she was the last of the Mothers of the Believers to die, passing away in Medina in 62 AH at about eighty-four, and was buried in al-Baqi.",
